WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:04.500
If Hollywood studios can make a franchise
out of a property, you can bet it's gonna

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:05.500
happen.

00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:08.940
Some become huge hits, like Fast &amp; Furious
and John Wick.

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:12.910
But every once in awhile, things just don't
work out, and there's no choice but to shut

00:00:12.910 --> 00:00:17.160
down production and move on — even if the
story hasn't reached its conclusion.

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:22.300
Here are a few movie franchises that ended
before their final chapter.

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:23.439
Eragon

00:00:23.439 --> 00:00:27.560
Before Twilight and The Hunger Games, there
was Eragon, based on the novel of the same

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:29.640
name by Christopher Paolini.

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:34.280
Released in 2006, the fantasy tale could have
been a four-film series, but it ended up serving

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:38.480
as a perfect case of a movie doing fairly
well at the box office while being ripped

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:42.760
apart by critics and audiences — and killing
the sequels before they started.

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:47.390
Paolini has written four novels in his Inheritance
Cycle series, which means there were three

00:00:47.390 --> 00:00:52.710
possible sequels for Eragon, and director
Stefan Fangmeier wanted to film them back-to-back.

00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:53.940
He told Movieweb,

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:58.820
"I think one would be best off to write the
third film first, and then, being happy with

00:00:58.820 --> 00:01:02.891
that, going back to the second one and doing
all the set-up work that will pay off in the

00:01:02.891 --> 00:01:03.891
third.

00:01:03.891 --> 00:01:07.960
Then probably filming two and three together
as they did with Pirates of the Caribbean;

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:09.790
as one production."

00:01:09.790 --> 00:01:14.790
Unfortunately, things didn't work out in his
favor, and Eragon's failure halted Fangmeier's

00:01:14.790 --> 00:01:17.670
directing career just as it was getting off
the ground.

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:21.780
Should he ever return to filmmaking, there
are a few fans of the books who'd still love

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:26.140
to see the complete Eragon saga unfold on
the big screen.

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:27.140
Sahara

00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:33.909
2005's Sahara looked great on paper: An Indiana
Jones-style adventure starring Matthew McConaughey

00:01:33.909 --> 00:01:37.979
and based on a best-selling novel by Clive
Cussler — it had all the ingredients to

00:01:37.979 --> 00:01:39.680
become a summer blockbuster.

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:45.740
Unfortunately, Sahara grossed a mere $119
million worldwide, despite the studio throwing

00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:48.430
$130 million into the project.

00:01:48.430 --> 00:01:52.740
It was that bloated budget that ultimately
brought the action to a standstill.

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:55.860
Director Breck Eisner had hoped to eventually
helm a sequel.

00:01:55.860 --> 00:02:00.299
But once Sahara hit the books as "one of the
biggest financial flops in Hollywood history,"

00:02:00.299 --> 00:02:04.280
according to the Los Angeles Times, any chance
of a sequel died with it.

00:02:04.280 --> 00:02:09.160
And with 18 novels in Cussler's Dirk Pitt
series to pull material from, Paramount had

00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:12.140
their hands on a treasure map marked with
a big red X.

00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:14.240
Well, you know what they say...

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:17.120
"An X never, ever marks the spot."

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:19.500
John Carter

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:24.660
It took over 80 years for Edgar Rice Burroughs'
classic novel A Princess of Mars to move from

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:31.000
print to screen in the form of 2012's John
Carter, and the payoff was...well, less than

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:32.000
spectacular.

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:36.580
When Disney reacquired the rights to the story,
the studio hired Finding Nemo and Wall-E director

00:02:36.580 --> 00:02:42.520
Andrew Stanton to helm the film and poured
over $300 million into the sci-fi extravaganza,

00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:46.900
clearly expecting the classic tale to rake
in boatloads at the box office.

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:48.950
Spoiler alert: It didn't.

00:02:48.950 --> 00:02:53.500
The movie was such a disaster that the head
of Walt Disney Studios, Rich Ross, actually

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:56.420
resigned his post after John Carter's release.

00:02:56.420 --> 00:02:59.560
Disney originally had plans for the film to
be the first in a trilogy:

00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:03.830
"When we started on this film we actually
wrote treatments for the first three films

00:03:03.830 --> 00:03:05.490
based on the first three books."

00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:09.410
Unfortunately, since the film flopped and
interest at Disney waned, the rights reverted

00:03:09.410 --> 00:03:14.880
to the Burroughs estate in 2014, shooting
down any hopes of John Carter 2 — at least

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:17.790
with the cast and crew of the first movie.

00:03:17.790 --> 00:03:19.600
Percy Jackson

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:24.950
In the late 2000s, 20th Century Fox hired
Chris Columbus to adapt Rick Riordan's Percy

00:03:24.950 --> 00:03:27.960
Jackson &amp; the Olympians series for the big
screen.

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:32.720
Columbus was the perfect choice for a young-adult
fantasy flick: after all, he directed Home

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:35.350
Alone and the first two Harry Potter movies.

00:03:35.350 --> 00:03:39.880
And while Percy Jackson &amp; the Olympians: The
Lightning Thief wasn't as big as Harry Potter,

00:03:39.880 --> 00:03:44.300
it was still a moderate success at the box
office, which was enough to justify a sequel.

00:03:44.300 --> 00:03:49.140
German filmmaker Thor Freudenthal came on
to helm the next installment, Percy Jackson:

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:52.570
The Sea of Monsters, which saw most of the
main cast return.

00:03:52.570 --> 00:03:57.640
The movie grossed almost as much as its predecessor,
but somewhere along the line, the studio pulled

00:03:57.640 --> 00:03:59.610
the plug on the third installment.

00:03:59.610 --> 00:04:04.120
While Percy Jackson actor Logan Lerman says
that fans are still hoping for a trilogy,

00:04:04.120 --> 00:04:08.900
he's not optimistic, telling Daily Nexus,
"I think we all are getting a little old to

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:12.840
play those characters, so I wouldn't hold
my breath."

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:14.630
I Am Number Four

00:04:14.630 --> 00:04:20.280
Although 2011's I Am Number Four grossed nearly
three times its production budget at the worldwide

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:27.160
box office, it only made $150 million — apparently
not enough to justify building a whole franchise.

00:04:27.160 --> 00:04:29.790
Its negative reviews surely didn't help, either.

00:04:29.790 --> 00:04:34.450
The Rotten Tomatoes critics consensus calls
the movie "a derivative and ultimately forgettable

00:04:34.450 --> 00:04:35.560
sci-fi thriller."

00:04:35.560 --> 00:04:41.040
Shortly after the film's release in 2011,
the film's co-writer Marti Noxon told Collider

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:45.260
that a sequel was put on the "back burner"
because the movie wasn't a big enough success

00:04:45.260 --> 00:04:46.560
at the box office.

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:48.600
In 2013, director D.J.

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:53.100
Caruso said he and the studios were discussing
adapting the series' second novel, The Power

00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:57.380
of Six, but nothing definitive ever seem to
come out if those talks.

00:04:57.380 --> 00:04:59.200
Divergent

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:03.350
As the Twilight gravy train started coming
to an end, Summit Entertainment looked to

00:05:03.350 --> 00:05:06.760
Veronica Roth's Divergent book series to fill
the void.

00:05:06.760 --> 00:05:11.770
The young adult dystopian series came gift-wrapped
with a strong fan base, and the first two

00:05:11.770 --> 00:05:15.020
films, Divergent and Insurgent, did well at
the box office.

00:05:15.020 --> 00:05:20.371
Then came the third installment, 2016's Allegiant,
which completely bombed and forced the studio

00:05:20.371 --> 00:05:23.270
to hold off on their plans for a two-part
finale.

00:05:23.270 --> 00:05:27.530
Stuck with a dying franchise that demanded
a huge production budget, Summit decided to

00:05:27.530 --> 00:05:32.940
change course and conclude the series by re-purposing
the final installment into a TV series.

00:05:32.940 --> 00:05:37.070
Unfortunately, the cast and crew weren't on
board with the idea, especially headliner

00:05:37.070 --> 00:05:41.560
Shailene Woodley, who told E! News that she
"didn't sign up for a TV show."

00:05:41.560 --> 00:05:46.330
In February 2017, Woodley said that she'd
officially backed out of the series, making

00:05:46.330 --> 00:05:48.460
the planned sequel all the more unlikely.

00:05:51.650 --> 00:05:53.240
Ender's Game

00:05:53.250 --> 00:05:58.350
Based on the novel of the same name by Orson
Scott Card, 2013's Ender's Game was given

00:05:58.350 --> 00:06:02.770
everything it needed to become a blockbuster,
which included nabbing a heavyweight cast

00:06:02.770 --> 00:06:07.950
consisting of Harrison Ford, Ben Kingsley,
Asa Butterfield, and Hailee Steinfeld.

00:06:07.950 --> 00:06:13.440
But although Ender's Game garnered generally
positive reviews, it only raked in $125.5

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:18.840
million against a production budget of $110
million, not nearly enough to push the film

00:06:18.840 --> 00:06:20.990
into sequel-worthy territory.

00:06:20.990 --> 00:06:25.320
Despite one cast member suggesting a script
based on Ender's Shadow, a parallel telling

00:06:25.320 --> 00:06:29.780
of Ender's Game's story, budget constraints
prevented the studio from moving forward with

00:06:29.780 --> 00:06:31.390
their planned franchise.

00:06:31.390 --> 00:06:36.430
The studio also considered a TV spinoff, but
those plans have also petered out.

00:06:36.430 --> 00:06:38.570
Looks like it's game over for Ender's Game.

00:06:38.570 --> 00:06:40.210
Thanks for watching!

00:06:40.210 --> 00:06:42.850
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:42.850 --> 00:06:45.840
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

